    "title": "<p><strong>Table&nbsp;2.05</strong> Histopathological characteristics of breast papillary neoplasms</p>",
    "legend": "",
    "source": "Imported from 5th edition<br><br>this is a modified version of a table that was published in the previous blue book We have modified its content and had it reviewed also by Megan Troxell and her colleagues of the papilloma chapter",
    "copyright": "",
    "tablehtml": "<table class=\"MsoNormalTable\" style=\"width: 697.9pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"0\" width=\"931\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\">\r\n<tbody>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Neoplasm</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Presentation</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Papillary architecture</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Epithelial cells</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; border: none; border-top: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; background: #8DB3E2; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Myoepithelial cells</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Intraductal papilloma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Single lesion (central papilloma) or multiple lesions (peripheral papillomas)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Generally broad, blunt fronds</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Heterogeneous non-neoplastic epithelial cell population: luminal cells, usual ductal hyperplasia, apocrine metaplasia and hyperplasia</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Present throughout and at periphery</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Papilloma with ADH or DCIS</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Single lesion (central papilloma) or multiple lesions (peripheral papillomas)</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Generally broad, blunt fronds</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Focal areas of epithelial cells with architectural and cytological features of ADH or DCIS (usually low-grade); background of heterogeneous non-neoplastic cell population</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Mostly present throughout and at periphery; may be attenuated or absent in areas of ADH/DCIS</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Papillary DCIS</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Multiple lesions</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Slender fronds, sometimes branching</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Entire lesion occupied by an epithelial cell population with architectural and cytological features of DCIS of low, intermediate, or rarely high nuclear grade; can grow as a single layer along thin fibrovascular stalks</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Absent in papillae; present in attenuated form at the periphery of ducts</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 4; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Encapsulated papillary carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Usually single lesion</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Numerous slender fronds, sometimes branching; peripheral, typically well-developed, fibrous capsule</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Entire lesion occupied by an epithelial cell population with architectural and cytological features of DCIS of low- or intermediate-grade; can grow as a single layer along thin fibrovascular stalks; cribriform, micropapillary, and solid patterns may be present, with fusion of adjacent papillae</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Usually absent throughout and at periphery</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 5; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Solid papillary carcinoma in&nbsp;situ </strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Single or multiple lesions with a distribution pattern consistent with an in&nbsp;situ process</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Solid nodules with rounded, well-circumscribed contours and inconspicuous delicate fibrovascular stalks</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Entire lesion occupied by an epithelial cell population with cytological features of low or intermediate nuclear grade, growing predominantly in a solid manner; epithelial cells may have a spindled appearance; neuroendocrine and mucinous differentiation is frequent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Present around the tumour nodules</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">OR</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Absent around tumour nodules with a distribution pattern consistent with an in&nbsp;situ process</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 6; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Solid papillary carcinoma with invasion</strong></p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Invasive solid papillary carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Single or multiple lesions with an irregular contour and infiltrative growth pattern</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">The invasive component usually has no definite papillary architecture</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Solid papillary carcinoma with invasion: the invasive component exhibits features of invasive breast carcinoma of no special type (NST), mucinous carcinoma, or other subtypes</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Invasive solid papillary carcinoma is composed entirely of solid/papillary tumour nodules with ragged contours creating a geographical jigsaw pattern within a desmoplastic stroma</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; background: #DAEEF3; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Absent</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 7; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; page-break-inside: avoid;\">\r\n<td style=\"width: 80.15pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"107\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\"><strong>Invasive papillary carcinoma</strong></p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 103.5pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"138\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Single lesion</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 133.7pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"178\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Infiltrating carcinoma with papillary morphology, including fibrovascular cores</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 276.45pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"369\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Low-, intermediate-, or rarely high-grade nuclear atypia</p>\r\n</td>\r\n<td style=\"width: 104.1pt; border: none; border-bottom: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-bottom-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" width=\"139\">\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">Absent throughout</p>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</tbody>\r\n</table>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">ADH, atypical ductal hyperplasia; DCIS, ductal carcinoma in&nbsp;situ.</p>\r\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\r\n<p class=\"TableText\">In rare cases, there are overlapping features between solid papillary carcinoma and encapsulated papillary carcinoma or between encapsulated papillary carcinoma and papillary DCIS, and it may not be possible to distinguish papillary carcinoma subtypes in every case.</p>",
    "imageUrl": null,
    "thumbnail": null
  },
